[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
The outage is not a physical link failure between router0 and router1. The 10.16.1.0/24 uplink runs on eth2 on both routers and is healthy: both ends are UP/LOWER_UP with IPs 10.16.1.1 (router0 eth2) and 10.16.1.2 (router1 eth2), and router0 successfully pings 10.16.1.2 with 0% loss. Instead, the affected component is routing control on router0: FRRouting (RIP) is stopped. Evidence: “ripd is not running” and systemd shows “frr.service … Active: inactive (dead)” on router0.

Mechanism: With FRR down, router0 is no longer learning or advertising remote prefixes. Hosts at Location A (e.g., PC4 defaulting to 172.16.10.1 on router0) can reach local subnets but receive ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable” from 172.16.10.1 when trying to reach remote networks (e.g., PC4→192.168.200.11). Router0’s routing table lacks 192.168.200.0/24 (and likely other remote nets), confirming missing IGP-learned routes. Meanwhile, router1 still has RIP routes via 10.16.1.1, illustrating the asymmetry.

Localization: router0, FRR/ripd service (control plane), not the data-plane uplink. The “serial” interface eth3 on router0 is down, but it is not the WAN link; the WAN to router1 is eth2 and is up.

Root cause: FRR (RIP) on router0 was left stopped after the maintenance window, preventing route exchange.

Corrective fix: Restart and enable FRR on router0 (systemctl start frr; ensure ripd is enabled), verify RIP neighborships and that routes (e.g., 192.168.200.0/24, 172.18.51.0/24) populate in the kernel table, then re-test end-to-end reachability. No physical interface repair is required. === END ===